1. NO COMPETING USE

You may not use the software to provide a product or service that competes with
the software, or with any other product or service the licensor provides using
the software.

You may not use the software to build a separate product that substitutes for
it. This applies whether you make that product available to others or deploy it
only inside your company.

A separate product substitutes for the software when its primary purpose is to
provide substantially the same core functionality as the software, rather than
to extend, integrate with, or operate the software.

Products and services compete even when they provide their functionality
through a different kind of interface, for a different technical platform, or in
a different programming language, and even when they are provided free of
charge. If you present a product as a practical substitute for the software, it
competes.

1.1 WHAT SECTION 1 DOES NOT RESTRICT

Running the software is always permitted. That includes running a modified
copy, changing it as much as you like, and serving everyone in your company from
a single instance, for as long as you like.

None of the following is a separate substitute for the software: a plugin, an
extension written for your own needs, an integration, an application built
around the software, a tool that consumes its output, or a modified deployment
of the software itself. A broader product that happens to contain some
functionality overlapping with the software is not a substitute for it unless
providing substantially the same core functionality is that product's primary
purpose.

SERVICES DELIVERED TO SOMEONE ELSE'S DEPLOYMENT ARE PERMITTED. Installing,
configuring, operating, migrating, training on, supporting or consulting about
the software, on a deployment that your client controls, is permitted - and
stays permitted even though the licensor offers such services itself. Section 1
does not restrict it.

PROFESSIONAL SERVICES PERFORMED ON YOUR OWN DEPLOYMENT ARE PERMITTED. Using your
own deployment of the software to analyze data belonging to a client, and
delivering the resulting analysis, reports, forecasts or advice to that client,
is permitted - and stays permitted even though the licensor offers such services
itself. Section 1 does not restrict it. What your client receives is your work
product, not the software.

What is not permitted is running the software as a service that you operate on
your client's behalf, on infrastructure you control, so that your client
receives access to the software rather than running it. That is a hosted or
managed service, and Part 2 prohibits it separately.

The line section 1 draws is between your deployment of the software, or a
deployment your client controls, and a separate product built out of it. The
line Part 2 draws is between a client who receives the output of the software
and a client who receives access to the software.

1.2 IF THE LICENSOR LATER BRINGS YOUR PRODUCT INTO COMPETITION

If you are using the software to provide a product or service that does not
compete, and the licensor afterwards releases a new version of the software, or
begins providing a new product or service built using the software, and that
brings your product or service into competition for the first time, then:

  - what you provide does not thereby become a violation of this license;
  - you may continue to use every version of the software released before the
    date of that release to provide it; and
  - you may not use any version released on or after that date to provide it.

This section exists to protect you. Without it, a release by the licensor could
put you in violation of this license overnight, through no act of your own.


2. WHAT SECTION 1 PROHIBITS STAYS PROHIBITED HOWEVER IT IS PRODUCED

A product or service that section 1 prohibits you from building or providing
remains prohibited regardless of how it was produced. It makes no difference
whether a person wrote it by hand, whether an automated system produced it, or
whether the software reached that system as input, as context, or as training
data.

The licensor expressly reserves the right to reproduce and extract the software,
and any part of it, for text and data mining, including for the training,
fine-tuning or evaluation of any automated or machine-learning system. This
reservation is made in respect of every copy of the software, however obtained.

2.1 WHAT SECTION 2 DOES NOT RESTRICT

Section 2 adds no restriction on tools. You may use any tool you like -
including a code assistant, a coding agent, or a model - to read, understand or
change the software for any purpose this license permits. Using a code assistant
to modify your own instance is permitted for exactly the same reason that
modifying it by hand is permitted.

Section 2 closes a route to what section 1 prohibits. It does not narrow
anything section 1 permits.


